ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-p1-1 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 1 Paragraph 1" ;
    doc:bbox "54.952999114990234,98.21752166748047,557.4090576171875,130.34619140625" ;
    doc:inSection ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-section-0 ;
    doc:pageNumber 1 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 1 ;
    doc:text "AGENTICT2S: Robust Text-to-SPARQL via Agentic Collaborative Reasoning over Heterogeneous Knowledge Graphs for the Circular Economy" ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/collaborative%20reasoning>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/collaborative%20reasoning%20heterogeneous>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/graphs%20circular>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/graphs%20circular%20economy>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/heterogeneous%20knowledge> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/AGENTICT2S>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Agentic%20Collaborative%20Reasoning>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Circular%20Economy>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Heterogeneous%20Knowledge%20Graphs>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Robust%20Text>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/SPARQL> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-page-1 .

ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-p1-2 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 1 Paragraph 2" ;
    doc:bbox "83.85100555419922,144.67080688476562,528.151123046875,172.068359375" ;
    doc:inSection ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-section-0 ;
    doc:pageNumber 1 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 2 ;
    doc:text "Yang Zhao* Chengxiao Dai∗ Wei Zhuo Chuan Fu Tan Yue Xiu Dusit Niyato Jonathan Low Zhaozhi Eugene H.Z. Ho Daren Tan Zong Loong" ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/dusit>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/dusit%20niyato>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/ho%20daren>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/ho%20daren%20tan>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/tan> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/Chengxiao%20Dai>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Dai%E2%88%97%20Wei%20Zhuo%20Chuan%20Fu>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Ho%20Daren%20Tan%20Zong%20Loong>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Tan%20Zong>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Wei%20Zhuo%20Chuan%20Fu%20Tan%20Yue%20Xiu%20Dusit%20Niyato%20Jonathan%20Low%20Zhaozhi%20Eugene>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Yang%20Zhao>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Yang%20Zhao%2A%20Chengxiao>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Zhaozhi%20Eugene> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-page-1 .

ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-p1-4 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 1 Paragraph 4" ;
    doc:bbox "319.5,218.40748596191406,558.0049438476562,602.6298217773438" ;
    doc:inSection ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-section-3 ;
    doc:pageNumber 1 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 4 ;
    doc:text "edge graphs (KGs), which offers improved precision and interpretability. However, existing systems often embed large subgraphs in prompts (Cui, Sun, and Hu 2024), increasing latency and exceeding context limits; rely on weakly supervised retrievers (Yasunaga et al. 2021); and couple retrieval, generation and validation into single-agent pipelines (He et al. 2024; Xu et al. 2024), reducing modularity and adaptability. In addition, even state-of-the-art models struggle to generate semantically correct SPARQL queries, particularly on unseen graphs (Meyer et al. 2024). SPARQL is a standardized query language for RDF-based KGs (W3C 2013), used to express structured queries involving logical conditions, joins, and filters. This work introduces AGENTICT2S, a modular framework for answering questions on heterogeneous KGs. The system decomposes a natural language query into sub-goals corresponding to reasoning operations such as entity lookup or comparison. Each subgoal is assigned to candidate graphs via schema alignment and retrieval history, translated into executable SPARQL using schema-guided decoding, and validated using symbolic checks and counterfactual consistency analysis. The architecture separates retrieval, generation, and verification into distinct agents to improve transparency, error isolation, and generalizability. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first formulation of text-to-SPARQL generation for KGQA over heterogeneous graphs as a multi-agent problem. The system consists of five agents, including subgoal decomposer, retriever allocator, SPARQL generator, dual-stage verifier, and result integrator, that coordinate to support compositional reasoning and robust query execution. The design is evaluated in the context of the circular economy, where reasoning over distributed emissions data and non-unified classification schemes requires schema-aware planning and verifiable synthesis (Atiku 2020)." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/et%20al>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/generation>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/graphs>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/retrieval%20generation>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/sparql> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/2013>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/2020>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/2021>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/2024>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/AGENTICT2S>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Atiku>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Cui%2C%20Sun>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/KGQA>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Meyer%20et%20al>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/RDF>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/RDF->,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/SPARQL>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/W3C>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Xu%20et%20al>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Yasunaga%20et%20al>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/first>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/five> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-page-1 .

ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-p1-5 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 1 Paragraph 5" ;
    doc:bbox "54.0,521.064697265625,292.5050048828125,684.8932495117188" ;
    doc:inSection ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-section-3 ;
    doc:pageNumber 1 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 5 ;
    doc:text "Large language models (LLMs) demonstrate strong performance in a range of natural language processing tasks (Min et al. 2023), but face limitations in knowledge-intensive settings. Because their outputs are based on pre-trained parameters, LLMs are prone to hallucination and may lack factual consistency (Ji et al. 2023). Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) addresses this by incorporating external information at inference time (Lewis et al. 2020), although most RAG systems operate over unstructured text, which lacks the semantic structure required for accurate multi-hop reasoning. These limitations are critical in the circular economy, where answering queries requires reasoning over multiple heterogeneous KGs with inconsistent schemas. Structured retrieval methods such as GraphRAG (Ji et al. 2024) have been proposed to integrate LLMs with knowl-" ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/et%20al>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/ji%20et>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/ji%20et%20al>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/limitations>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/llms> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/2020>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/2023>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/2024>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/GraphRAG>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Ji%20et%20al>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Lewis%20et%20al>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/RAG> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-page-1 .

ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-p1-6 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 1 Paragraph 6" ;
    doc:bbox "323.9830322265625,628.690185546875,558.0044555664062,704.4067993164062" ;
    doc:inSection ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-section-3 ;
    doc:pageNumber 1 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 6 ;
    doc:text "• This work formulates cross-graph question answering over heterogeneous KGs as a modular multi-agent process. The design addresses the limitations of monolithic text-to-SPARQL pipelines by separating retrieval, synthesis, and verification into distinct composable agents. This decomposition allows for interpretable reasoning, schema-level adaptability, and improved fault isolation." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/composable%20agents>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/composable%20agents%20decomposition>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/distinct%20composable>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/distinct%20composable%20agents>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/improved%20fault> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/SPARQL> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-page-1 .

ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-p2-2 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 2 Paragraph 2" ;
    doc:bbox "319.5,56.17655944824219,558.0051879882812,234.02554321289062" ;
    doc:inSection ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-section-3 ;
    doc:pageNumber 2 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 2 ;
    doc:text "it reflects the intent of the question q and returns a complete and correct answer set A ⊆Ent, where Ent is the union of entity sets across all graphs in G. This task presents several challenges. The graphs in G may differ in schema, vocabulary, and structural representation, introducing substantial heterogeneity. Answering a question may require distributed reasoning, where information must be aggregated from multiple KGs. Furthermore, many KGQA benchmarks lack the gold-standard SPARQL annotations, limiting the feasibility of supervised learning. Finally, practical systems must meet efficiency requirements, making retrieval and query generation time sensitive. The objective is to develop a system that, given an input question q and a collection G of KGs, produces a query s such that executing s on the appropriate subset of G yields a correct and complete answer set A." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/answer%20set>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/complete>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/correct>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/ent>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/question> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/Ent>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/KGQA>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/SPARQL> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-page-2 .

ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-p2-4 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 2 Paragraph 4" ;
    doc:bbox "319.5,484.6301574707031,557.9995727539062,506.9000549316406" ;
    doc:inSection ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-section-4 ;
    doc:pageNumber 2 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 4 ;
    doc:text "Algorithm 1: AGENTICT2S Pipeline for Compositional KGQA" ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/algorithm>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/compositional>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/compositional%20kgqa>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/kgqa>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/pipeline> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/AGENTICT2S>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Algorithm%201%3A%20AGENTICT2S%20Pipeline%20for%20Compositional>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/KGQA> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-page-2 .

ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-p2-5 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 2 Paragraph 5" ;
    doc:bbox "319.4999694824219,511.5868225097656,558.0018920898438,543.8270263671875" ;
    doc:inSection ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-section-4 ;
    doc:pageNumber 2 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 5 ;
    doc:text "Require: Natural language question q, KGs G = G1, G2, . . . , Gn Ensure: Final answer set A" ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/ensure%20final>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/ensure%20final%20answer>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/final%20answer>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/final%20answer%20set>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/gn%20ensure> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/G1>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/G2>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Gn%20Ensure> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-page-2 .

ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-p2-6 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 2 Paragraph 6" ;
    doc:bbox "54.0,201.56805419921875,292.5046691894531,556.8117065429688" ;
    doc:inSection ex:agenticframeworkfort2spar-andut4q-section-4 ;
    doc:pageNumber 2 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 6 ;
    doc:text "Related Work RAG methods incorporate external information to improve the factual consistency of the language model output (Lewis et al. 2020; Gao et al. 2023). While effective for unstructured question answering, they assume flat input formats and are not well suited for tasks involving structured queries, multihop reasoning, or schema constraints. GraphRAG (Edge et al. 2024; Wu et al. 2024a) and related methods extend RAG by converting local graph neighborhoods into textual prompts. These methods retain some structural context but are limited by prompt length, formatting dependencies, and an assumption of a single underlying graph. Most also lack validation mechanisms for generated queries (Wang et al. 2024). Other work decomposes question answering into modular components for retrieval, reasoning, and execution (Tran et al. 2025; Barbosa, Santos, and Novais 2024). Multi-agent systems extend this idea by assigning subtasks to separate agents (Guo et al. 2024; Yang et al. 2025). For example, ReAgent (Zhao et al. 2025) applies local and global backtracking in multi-hop QA over text. These systems are typically applied to unstructured input and do not address structured query generation. General purpose agent frameworks such as ReAct (Yao et al. 2023), AutoGen (Wu et al. 2024b), CAMEL (Li et al. 2023), and MetaGPT (Hong et al. 2023) implement rolebased decomposition for planning and tool interaction across tasks. These methods have been applied to information retrieval, decision-making, and code synthesis in unstructured or semi-structured settings. They are not designed for structured KGQA and lack support for schema-aware reasoning or symbolic query verification." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/applied>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/et%20al>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/extend>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/methods>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/unstructured> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/2020>,
